in the seaside county of Ventura  California a storm is forming on the  horizon you won't see it on any weather  maps or meteorological charts you'll  find it brewing in the pages of the  county budget were hundreds of millions  of dollars in pension debt threatened to  drown the area the pension system right  now has a billion dollars unfunded  liability that's just simply  unsustainable we've seen the damage  these financial downpours can bring  higher taxes reduced public services and  in some cases bankruptcy so when the  winds began to change Ventura residents  left into action to enact reform in  November's midterm elections only to be  stopped by a judge before they could get  to the ballot box the faculty matters  they have huge liabilities hundreds of  millions of dollars and if they don't  address these liabilities now future  services will be cut why did reform fail  and why is it so hard to accomplish in  the first place what's stopping  governments from saving themselves from  a predictable flood of debt the county  has big guns but we're finding that the  Union together with the people have  bigger guns  the story of Venters failed pension  reform begins in 1947 when residents  elected to create the Ventura County  Employees Retirement Association also  known as vecera as the country emerged  from the Great Depression state saw  benefit in establishing retirement  systems that could act as a safety net  for workers to avoid poverty later in  life in California a 1937 lobby of  counties a choice they could become part  of the statewide retirement system or  they could create their own program 20  counties including Los Angeles San Diego  and Ventura chose the latter became  known as 37 act counties this  designation is important because if  Ventura voters could elect change it  could serve as a blueprint for the  remaining 37 act counties to enact  pension reform without having to go  through the legislative quagmire in  Sacramento since the 1940s the sara has  grown to cover some 16,000 County  government employees the fund is a  defined benefit program meaning that the  employer or in this case the county  promises their workers a fixed sum of  money when they retire for the rest of  their lives since defined benefit  systems provide retirement benefits that  are predetermined the county needs to  make sure they set aside enough money  each year to pay for future retirees  they do this by placing contributions  into a collective pension fund where  money is that invested to generate  revenue to cover future benefits of  investments consistently hit or exceed  their mark the county has no extra cost  but if they fall short of expected  returns as many do the taxpayer is  responsible not only for the cost of the  workers pension but for the cost of  additional debt payments as well the  government worker contributes just a  small fraction of their paycheck toward  the retirement fund for general county  workers that number is around seven  percent while public safety employees  contribute about fifteen percent the  taxpayers match those contributions and  pick up the payments for any debt that  has accrued after 35 years of service  the average county employee in Ventura  with an 85 thousand dollar salary can  expect to retire with a little over  74,000  and annual benefits on top of their  social security and 401k pay the average  public safety employee making a hundred  and twenty five thousand dollars a year  can expect to walk away with an annual  pension of a hundred thousand dollars  after 30 years of service you know when  you look at compensation and pension  particularly of the safety and the top  executives in county we're right up  there if not higher than anybody else  though the stewards of the pension fund  have invested responsibly they began to  notice a disturbing trend Bill Wilson  who has served on the Retirement Board  for over 16 years explains we've been  earning very good returns ever since  1994 despite that here we are with a  billion dollars in unfunded liability  you can't tell me that's right the  recent economic recession hasn't helped  veceras bottom line but it doesn't fully  explain why the system is so far in the  red in 1999 pension payments accounted  for just one percent of the total budget  today that number is seventeen percent  as retirement eat up a bigger portion of  the budget the amount residents have had  to kick in to cover pension obligations  has also increased today taxpayers pay a  hundred and fifty three million dollars  per year to the pension system that's  triple the number they paid out over a  decade ago in the next five years that  number is expected to climb to 226  million some of the rising costs can be  attributed to demographics retirees are  living longer and drawing more in  pension payments unrealistic investment  targets have also hampered growth in the  retirement system while Ventura assumes  the standard seven point seven five  percent return on retirement investments  the county has seen just a 5.8 two  percent return in the last five years in  addition to these factors the county  also has the distinction of having some  of the highest retirement benefits in  the state thanks to a practice known as  spiking in which retirees can manipulate  their final pay with supplemental  benefits to boost their pensions a 1997  state Supreme Court ruling known as  livin  her decision upheld this practice which  legitimized pension spiking throughout  the state after the ruling stories of  outrageous pensions began to make their  way into the headlines in 2012 it was  discovered that County Executive Marty  Robinson had added thousands of dollars  of unused sick pay and bonuses to spike  her final pension pay over forty  thousand dollars more a year more  recently former County Sheriff Bob  Brooks made headlines for his suit  against the county he is asking for an  additional 75 thousand dollars on top of  his more than quarter million annual  pension it's unjust the taxpayers bear a  large burden for the government  employees pension and these pensions are  much more generous than is available for  them in the course you have to ask is  what's so special about government  employees that they shouldn't have to  assume some of the own risk like the  taxpayer for their retirement as citizen  outrage grew over this disparity the  Ventura County taxpayers association  took action and crafted a pension reform  initiative of which the reason  foundation the nonprofit that publishes  reason TV produced some analysis this  measure does three things it moves all  public employees in the county including  public safety from defined benefit to  defined contribution system it also  freezes pay for five years to keep those  pension costs level and additionally it  guarantees disability and death benefits  for any public safety officials to put  themselves in land duty we've got an  independent actuary and we expect to  save 460 million dollars in cash over 15  years and it will reduce the pension  system liability by one point eight  billion dollars so it's a significant  savings and you know that just creates  opportunities to do you know expanded  services or even you know explore some  new ways to serve the residents of the  county Richard Thompson leads the  Ventura County taxpayers association and  says his group has spent nearly three  years working on the reform initiative  the measure would only apply to new  employees hired after july 2015 under  the plan the county would contribute no  more than four percent of compensation  for general employees and eleven percent  for fire and safety personnel the plan  would also combat the effects of spiking  by placing a cap on the incomes used to  calculate pensions for five years it  will strengthen the promises that were  made to retirees because rather than  this growing pension debt it will it  actually saves money and so those people  that are already retired and those  people that are working today will have  greater confidence in a pension system  that once again isn't paying for itself  the plan was popular with local  residents reformers gathered over 46 of  the needed 21 thousand signatures to put  the measure on the ballot but the  initiative was met with a litany of  resistance from the usual suspects that  oppose reform unions and politicians who  are both bankrolled by the unions and  benefit from rich retirement benefits  public employees turned out in droves to  county board meetings sporting gear  provided to them by local union groups  to voice their opposition some even  showed up at petition drives to  intimidate people from signing union  heads also put pressure on County  Supervisors to delay the reappointment  of Peter Foy the loan supervisor  supporting reform to the Retirement  Board union leaders argue that the  problems of pension debt are being blown  out of proportion they say moving to a  defined contribution plan would put the  public at risk because it would be  difficult to recruit qualified  government workers who often accept  lower pay for better benefits for Sylvia  Ramirez a retiree who's a member of the  local SEIU chapter the proposed pension  plan would bring financial independence  that she thinks people aren't ready for  the 401k is not enough I couldn't afford  it I couldn't afford that money that was  going to take bread and milk away from  my table on that pay day when I was busy  paying my bills but I always knew I had  the security of my pension  people are not as diligent about saving  they're not as understanding they don't  have the knowledge to make the right  choices on which where they want their  monies to go I never could after heated  debate throughout the summer the board  finally agreed to place pension reform  on the ballot but their actions were  made moot after a county judge  preemptively ruled the measure illegal  stating that the existing law under the  1937 act does not permit an individual  county to opt out or terminate its  participation in the pension system  through a countywide vote according to  the judge's ruling even though voters  elected to create a pension fund decades  ago the law provides them no way to exit  the system through a vote reformers  would have to either repeal or amend the  law through state legislation to change  their costly pension programs though the  ruling was a setback the Ventura County  taxpayers association plans to fight for  statewide action and recent Court  rulings that may allow bankrupt cities  like Stockton to restructure their  pension debt could pave another pathway  for reform but before real change can  occur local governments need to  acknowledge the problems in their  retirement systems before their cities  face an inescapable deluge of debt
 

